// case: at-out-of-range
// what: an index past the end of a string is not a character. `orbit_string_at`
//      (`runtime/collections.c:369-373`) answers 0 for `index >= len`, which is
//      indistinguishable from the character NUL, and the front end has both the
//      receiver and the index and raises nothing. Measured: `s.at(99)` over
//      "hello" prints 0. The receiver here is a real string, so this isolates
//      the missing bounds check from n31's receiver-type bug: with the receiver
//      correct, an in-range index is right and an out-of-range one is silently
//      zero.
//
//      Nothing in std/ depends on this being 0 -- `getOr` guards with
//      `i >= l.len()` before it calls `get` -- so it can be made an error
//      without a coordinated std change. FINDINGS F-0014.
// known-defect: F-0014
// wrong-value: 0
fn main() -> int {
    val s = "hello"
    print(s.at(99))
    return 0
}
